3. The growing proportion of elderly individuals worldwide has intensified the need for

effective, non-intrusive systems that support independent living and ensure continuous

monitoring of functional health. Difficulties in Activities of Daily Living (ADLs) are

strong indicators of declining autonomy and predictors of institutional care,

hospitalization, and overall health deterioration. Traditional monitoring approaches

including caregiver observation, self reports, and wearable devices suffer from limitations

such as reporting bias, user discomfort, and inconsistent compliance, making them

unsuitable for long term and real world deployment.

This report investigates a multimodal sensing framework for Human Activity

Recognition (HAR) in eldercare environments, integrating radar sensing

(AWR6843AOP), pressure sensitive flooring (Sensfloor), physiological monitoring

(SOMNOtouch™ NIBP), and markerless motion capture (Captury). A structured

laboratory setup was designed to emulate nursing home conditions, enabling

standardized data acquisition across diverse ADL related activities.

A comprehensive analysis of existing HAR datasets and sensing modalities highlights

the tradeoffs between accuracy, privacy, and usability, motivating the development of a

hybrid sensing approach. The collected data provides a foundation for evaluating sensor

performance, understanding human movement patterns in realistic ADL settings, and

exploring the potential for automated activity monitoring in eldercare



