2. Multi view stereo and 3D Reconstruction he goal of MVS is to generate a complete, dense 3D model from calibrated images—a

task critical to fields ranging from medical imaging, where precise modeling of

anatomical structures aids diagnostics (e.g., lesions or orthopedic implants), to cultural

heritage preservation, where digital archiving safeguards fragile artifacts. Despite rapid

advancements in MVS algorithms, challenges persist. Variations in lighting, occlusions,



and textureless regions complicate depth estimation, while the scarcity of standardized

benchmarks hampers objective performance comparisons.

This work addresses these challenges through a robust pipeline combining

COLMAP-based SfM for sparse reconstruction, graph-cut optimization to refine depth

maps, and MVS to fuse refined data into a dense point cloud. By integrating geometric

priors and photometric consistency, our approach mitigates ambiguities inherent in

single-view analysis, enabling accurate reconstructions even with unordered image

collections. The implications are profound: from enabling virtual restoration of

archaeological sites to enhancing robotic perception, this methodology advances the

frontier of 3D vision, bridging the gap between the physical and digital realms



